 ],
 "literal": "If one does great good, are there even those who do not cherish them? / Even if she possesses only one good quality, with great love, / to end her sulking, please show grace! Eating leaves, / on the cool, fragrant mountain slope where beautiful, straight bamboos grow, / the soft-walking wild cow sleeps, / O lord of the good mountain land! She has no one except you.",
 "translation": "If one does great good, is there anyone who would not cherish them? Even if she has but a single good quality, please show her grace with abundant love so that her sulking may end. O lord of the fertile mountain country, where the soft-walking wild cow eats leaves and sleeps on the cool, fragrant slope amidst tall, beautiful bamboos! She has no one else but you.",
 "modern": "பெரிய நன்மைகளைச் செய்தால், ஒருவரைப் போற்றாதவர் எவரேனும் உண்டோ? அவளிடம் ஒரே ஒரு நற்குணம் மட்டுமே இருந்தாலும், உமது அன்பு மேம்பட்டு அவளது ஊடல் தீருமாறு அவளுக்கு அருள் செய்வீராக. இலைகளைத் தின்று, அழகிய மூங்கில்கள் வளர்ந்துள்ள குளிர்ந்த நறுமணமிக்க மலைச்சாரலில், மெல்ல நடக்கும் காட்டுப்பசு உறங்கும் நல்ல மலைநாட்டிற்குத் தலைவனே! உன்னைத் தவிர வேறு புகலிடம் அவளுக்கு இல்லை.",
 "explanation": "The confidante speaks to the hero who has returned after a period of separation, urging him to appease the heroine who is sulking. She begins with a general truth: anyone would cherish a person who does great good. She then pleads that even if the heroine has only one good quality, the hero should show her great love to resolve her distress. The poem is set in the Kuriñci landscape, symbolized by the mountain slopes where tall bamboos grow and wild cows rest. The image of the wild cow eating leaves and sleeping peacefully on the cool slope represents the safety and comfort the heroine should find in the hero's embrace. By addressing him as the lord of this fertile land, the confidante reminds him of his duty to provide that same shelter and peace to the heroine, who has no one else but him.",
